  "preamble": "Deaffrication of the voiced alveolar affricate [dz] to the fricative [z], through loss of the stop closure. It happened independently in medieval Romance (Old French, Galician-Portuguese and Old Spanish all lost their dental affricates) and in most Slavic languages, where the [dz] produced by the second and third palatalizations of *g became [z] everywhere except Polish, Slovak and early Old Church Slavonic.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deaffrication, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "French",
      "languageFamily": "Gallo-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old French doze 'twelve' (z = /dz/; < Latin duodecim)",
          "to": "Middle French douze [duz]",
          "note": "The affricates /ts dz tʃ dʒ/ became the fricatives [s z ʃ ʒ] by Middle French; Old French wrote /dz/ as z."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Portuguese",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Galician-Portuguese fazer [faˈdzer] 'to do'",
          "to": "Portuguese fazer [fɐˈzeɾ]",
          "note": "Portuguese, like Old Spanish, deaffricated /ts/ and /dz/ to lamino-alveolar fricatives, later merged with the apical ones in most dialects."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Russian",
      "languageFamily": "East Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *kъnędzь 'prince'; Old Church Slavonic кънѧѕь kŭnędzĭ",
          "to": "Russian князь knjazʹ; also OCS кънѧзь kŭnęzĭ",
          "note": "The *dz from the third palatalization became z; Polish ksiądz keeps the affricate. Czech kněz and Serbo-Croatian knȇz show the same z."
        }
      ]
    },
